[Event "Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/18643180/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "8.2"]
[White "Q&A"]
[Black "Why do I not recommend CCT?"]
[Result "*"]

 { I am sure many of you have heard that candidate moves should strictly be calculated in this order: Checks, captures, and threats (also known as CCT) . This is because tactics often arise from these forcing moves. However, there are a few downsides to adopting this method as our primary calculation framework. For one, if the best move happened to be a “quiet” threat, much effort would be wasted calculating irrelevant checks and captures. Even worse, if the best move is defensive, then you are barking up the wrong tree if you consider only checks, captures, and threats. Furthermore, there are plenty of useless checks that shouldn't occupy our attention at all. Let's look at an example. } 1. { -KEY- } e4 d5 { We should not waste any time calculating } 2. Bb5+ $4 { because Black can solidify the centre while gaining a tempo. } c6 { Hence, to avoid any 'false alarms', I recommend identifying the exact preconditions to tactics such as unprotected pieces, trappable pieces, etc. After all, the presence of checks, captures, and threats, do not indicate tactics as reliably as the presence of tactical motifs. } 
 *

[Event "Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/18643182/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "8.3"]
[White "Q&A"]
[Black "How do we distinguish between hanging and unprotected pieces?"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "1r5k/1r3pp1/pP2p2p/P5Q1/7P/8/5PP1/7K w - - 0 1"]

 { ‘Is a Queen that is defended ONCE and attacked ONCE by a pawn still considered an unprotected piece?’ ‘Is a pawn attacked by TWO Rooks and defended by ONE pawn still considered a hanging piece?’ ‘I saw a piece that has ONE direct high-valued defender, TWO X-ray attackers, ONE low-valued X-ray defender, is within forking range of this Knight, and is on a critical diagonal, and … … Is it hanging or unprotected??’ I have received many variants of such questions. In a practical setting, the definition of hanging and unprotected pieces is not as important as recognising the threats and potential threats lurking behind. As a general guide, a hanging piece is clearly under threat while an unprotected piece can potentially be under threat. Take for example, the g5 Queen defended once and attacked once by an enemy pawn. It is, by definition, unprotected because it has equal number of attackers and defenders. However, given the Queen’s exceptionally high value, it is under threat and therefore effectively hanging. } 1. { -KEY- } Qe5 { Consider another scenario where the b6 pawn is defended by another pawn and attacked by a two Rooks. With two attackers to one defender, the pawn is hanging by definition. However, at stake here is two mere pawns for a Rook. This means ...Rxb6 is a non-starter and so the pawn is not under any real threat. } 
 *

[Event "Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/18643183/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "8.4"]
[White "Q&A"]
[Black "Why are they called 'unprotected' pieces?"]
[Result "*"]

 { Why do I call a piece, with say 2 attackers and 2 defenders, ‘unprotected’ (may also be called ‘undefended’) ? To understand why, we need to consider the most straightforward definition of an unprotected piece and its implication. An unprotected piece is a piece that is not protected (i.e., 0 defenders) . It should also have 0 attackers. Why? The moment it has one or more attackers, it would be classified as a hanging piece instead. The reason unprotected pieces are even worth identifying is that they are one attacker away from becoming hanging pieces. Since pieces with an equal number of attackers and defenders share this key characteristic, such pieces should also be considered unprotected. If you’d like to see how pieces with 0 attackers and 0 defenders are categorically the same as those with an equal number of attackers as defenders, I refer you to this page By the way, this definition of unprotected/undefended pieces is shared by renowned chess instructors. Here are a couple of examples - Simply copy & paste the link and it will take you to the exact timestamp in the video: National Master ‘Jerry’ from ChessNetwork defining unprotected pieces: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=E5XmaAshsB4 International Master John Bartholomew referring to the h4 Rook (with 0 attackers and 0 defenders) as undefended: https://youtu.be/Ao9iOeK_jvU?t=2756 Grandmaster Daniel Naroditsky labelling the g1 Rook (with one attacker and one defender) as undefended: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K8QMjqu0_MY&t=2060s }  
 *

