[White "Q&A"]
[Black "How do we apply calculation techniques to fast games?"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "6k1/5ppp/8/8/7P/6P1/r4P2/2R3K1 w - - 0 1"]

 { How do we apply a lengthy thought process such as the Burger Technique to fast games and how do we know when to use it? The Burger Technique is largely about identifying motifs. Tactical motifs not only tell us where to look for tactics, but also when to look for tactics. If there are no motifs, we don’t bother looking for tactics. On the other hand, if there is even just one unprotected piece on board, we should seek to exploit it. This means that you need to always remain alert to all tactical motifs to know when to spot tactics. For most, this requires too much effort and time. Therefore, we need to develop an unconscious competency to spot such motifs. You can start by using the Burger Technique slowly and deliberately on untimed puzzles. This will strengthen your intuition to spot tactical motifs. Eventually, this will become such second nature that you will know to go for the kill as soon as a motif presents itself, even in fast games. Not convinced this is possible? Let’s consider a simple chess skill you have completely mastered and turn the question around – How do you know when to look out for back rank mates in your fast games? Well, you’ve simply gotten so good at it that you are not only able to find it in puzzles, but also know to execute it when they present themselves. You don’t even need to think about when to look out for back rank mates. In the same way, repeated practice using the Burger Technique sharpens your intuition to spot other motifs such as trappable pieces and critical squares just as quickly and comfortably as you would for back rank mates. For an example of this in action, I refer you to the conclusion of Chapter 1. } 1. { -KEY- } Rc8# 
