[Event "Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/18643186/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "8.7"]
[White "Q&A"]
[Black "Superposition vs Reciprocal Thinking"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "1k3r2/6RP/1p5n/7P/P1P5/1P6/2P2p2/5K2 b - - 1 1"]

 { You may think that the Superposition Technique is overly complicated and is therefore always less preferred over Reciprocal Thinking. However, there are positions where Reciprocal Thinking proves arduous and the best way to tackle them is to use the Superposition Technique. As an example, let's revisit Puzzle #21 from Chapter 2 and solve it using Reciprocal Thinking instead of the Superposition Technique. To recap, Reciprocal Thinking requires us to first calculate one line and diagnose the cause of failure if it doesn't work. Let's suppose the following line is the first line we consider. } 1... { -KEY- } Nf5 2. Rf7 Ng3+ 3. Kxf2 Rxf7+ 4. Kxg3 Rxh7 5. Kg4 { After calculating through this first line, Reciprocal Thinking hits a roadblock. There are several possible causes of failures, and it is challenging to pinpoint exactly the wrong move (s) . Any of the following diagnoses are plausible: - White's h5 pawn is the cause of failure and we should consider ways to get rid of it ahead of time. - The reason we can't win is that we lost our f-pawn, and we should figure a line that preserves it. - We could have given White's King an extra tempo somewhere along the line, allowing White to defend the h-pawn just in the nick of time. Let's reset the position and use the superposition technique instead. } ( 1. Kg4 Nf5 2. Rf7 Ne3 { Here, we recognise that Ng3+ is similar, so we calculate both lines at once. } 3. Kxf2 Rxf7 4. Kxe3 { Had we played 2...Ng3+, White's King would be on g3. } Rxh7 { At the end of the day, it's easy to see we much rather have White's King on e3 rather than g3, so 2...Ne3+ is the winning move. } ) 
  { As you can see, the Superposition Technique is much better suited than Reciprocal Thinking here because it draws emphasis to the 'Ng3+ or Ne3+' dilemma. In summary, in positions with multiple similar candidate moves, it is better to use the Superposition Technique because: 1. You only need to calculate one line, which speeds up your calculation. 2. Comparison between the variations is much clearer because you can see the all the resulting positions at once. 3. It draws attention to the critical move where the branch occurs. However, the advantage of Reciprocal Thinking over the Superposition Technique should not be understated: 1. It can be used in a much wider range of positions, and does not have the requirement for multiple similar candidate moves. 2. It can help you find candidate moves that were not even on your radar. } 
