[Round "8.8"]
[White "Q&A"]
[Black "How do we review this course? #1"]
[Result "*"]

 { To explain how to review the course, I must first explain the importance of reviewing your mistakes. The biggest reason many chess players fall short of their full potential is that they review their mistakes ineffectively. From my observation, after a typical player notices a missed tactic in the post-game analysis, they might simply find out why the engine’s suggestion is better, make a brief conclusion, and never take a second look at it. The problem with such an approach is that minimal learning takes place. Instead, you should analyse your mistakes methodically by asking yourself the key question, ‘What could I have done to avoid this mistake?’. This answer can usually be found using the techniques taught in this course. When articulated concisely, it will strengthen your tactical intuition and increase your chances of finding similar tactics when they present themselves in real games. To see this review process in action, I will give you a moderately challenging puzzle (compared to what we have seen in this course so far) on the next page. Do not be too disheartened if you are unable to solve it promptly. In any case, we will review the puzzle together and I will show how you can maximise your learning through the process. }  
 *

