[Event "Calculation: A Complete Guide for Tournament Players"]
[Site "https://www.chessable.com/variation/18643189/"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "8.10"]
[White "Q&A"]
[Black "How do we review this course? #3"]
[Result "*"]
[FEN "rnb1kbnr/pp2pppp/8/3P4/8/q4N2/P2B1PPP/1R1QKB1R w Kkq - 1 1"]

 { The way to approach this puzzle is to use the Burger Technique and recognise 2 key motifs - The unprotected c8 Bishop, and the trappable a3 Queen, who only has the squares a2, a3, c5, and d6. The closest way to trapping the Queen is 1. Bb4, which covers all the squares except a2. Therefore, we should look for a move that defends a2 with tempo. And what better way to do it by attacking c8? } 1. { -KEY- } Qc2 { We begin by visualising 1. Qc2, which 'forks' a2 and c8. But before rushing to play it, we should likewise systematically find a defence for Black. We can do this by applying the Burger Technique from Black's perspective - How can Black save c8 and the Queen simultaneously? } Bd7 { Saving the Bishop while creating an a4 escape square for the Queen. Now it is time for us to use the Burger Technique for White again. It remains that trapping Black's Queen is a key motif, but another one is the critical c8 square. If not for the d7 Bishop, Qc8 is mate! So we look for a move that wins back the a4 square while wrestling with the d7 Bishop for the c8 square. } ( 1... Nd7 { This saves the Bishop but not the Queen } 2. Bb4 ) 
 ( 1... Na6 2. Bb5 Bd7 { Transposes to the main line } ( 2... Kd8 { With Black's King stuck in the middle of an open board, any natural move by White wins here, but the most direct is } 3. Ne5 $1 { And we are crashing through on f7 } ) 
 ) 
2. Bb5 { Threatening Qc8# and Bb4 to trap the Black Queen. Using the Burger Technique for Black again, we need to find a move that defends c8 while preventing Bb4. } Na6 { Covering c8 and b4 } 3. Bxd7+ { The rest of the combination is rather straightforward. After all, it is often a good idea to draw the enemy King to the centre with heavy pieces controlling the adjacent open files. } Kxd7 4. Rxb7+ Ke8 5. Qc6+ Kd8 6. Qd7# { I expect you would have taken at least a minute or two to read through how I arrived at the correct moves. My challenge to you now is to return to the starting position and repeat the entire problem-solving process from memory. Articulate it aloud and use a stopwatch to time yourself. Remember, the task is not simply to regurgitate the correct moves, but to really follow each step in the problem-solving process. Be sure to include all the motifs, including recognising the trappable Queen, unprotected c8 Bishop, and thereafter, the critical c8 square for your Queen, and so on until you arrive at checkmate. Once you are done, repeat it again and aim to beat your previous timing before continuing to read from here. _________________________________ I’m sure your second run was faster than your first. With enough repetition, you will need less and less time to explain the tactic. At some point, you mind will run faster than your mouth can. This is when you have truly understood and internalised the tactic. And that’s how you review your mistakes. Then how do we review this course? Or any course for that matter? When Chessable calls you to review your moves, it is important to avoid playing from memory alone. Instead, you should reconstruct your analysis and reason your way to the memorised move just like what we have done for this puzzle. This ensures that you are reviewing both the move, and the REASON that move should be played. So, when I see you back here in 4 hours for the review, don’t just rush to play out the moves, but really repeat my entire thought process for this puzzle. And do the same for all your Chessable courses. It will slow you down a lot, but the work you put in will return you massive dividends. } 
