[FEN "r4rk1/1b1p1ppp/ppn3n1/4qNBQ/2B1P3/2P5/P1P3PP/R4RK1 w - - 0 1"]

1. { -KEY- } Bf6 $3 Qc5+ ( 1... Qxf6 2. Nh6 { Discovered attack on Black's Queen } gxh6 3. Rxf6 ) 
 ( 1... gxf6 2. Qh6 { with Qg7# to follow. } ) 
2. Kh1 Qxc4 3. Qh6 $3 { Threatening Qxg7# } gxh6 4. Nxh6# { This tactic from the game Ove Kinnmark vs Strem is practically impossible to solve by identifying conventional tactical motifs (such as unprotected pieces and trappable pieces) alone. You could never arrive at the right move without knowing this exact pattern. So, what are we to do? There is no other way than to go through this pattern repeatedly and internalise it. This means executing PIE on this position so that similar puzzles become solvable in the future. And if you think that this pattern is too obscure to be worth internalising, have a look at the game Vasilios Kotronias vs Daniel John King. It goes to show patterns do repeat more often than we realise and that we should internalise every new pattern with utmost seriousness. In so doing, you expand your repertoire of tactical motifs and can handle a wider range of positions. } 
