[Black "Course Conclusion"]
[Result "*"]

 { Summarising the entire course, here is a guide to your chess calculation training: WHILE SOLVING PUZZLES OR PLAYING GAMES: 1. Whenever you are solving puzzles or playing slow games, conscientiously execute the steps of the Burger Technique. 2. In these puzzles or games, you may incorporate various forms of reciprocal thinking (typical reciprocal thinking, identifying the King’s escape squares, and the Superposition Technique) in step 3 of the Burger Technique as you deem fit. 3. For endgame positions, you may incorporate various endgame tactical ideas discussed in Chapter 5. With enough practice doing 1. to 3., you will start to instinctively execute the calculation techniques in real games when it is called for. WHILE STUDYING CHESS: 4. Whenever you realise you made a mistake, ask yourself ‘What could I have done to avoid this mistake?’. After you obtain a precise and concise answer, repeat it to yourself several times until you feel confident in your understanding of the tactic. 5. If you feel like you found a tactic far too slowly, ask yourself ‘How could I have arrived at the solution faster?’ and follow the same process as 4. 6. If there is no reasonable way to arrive at the right move, use the four steps of PIE to internalise the combination. 7. When reviewing moves on Chessable, remind yourself of why the memorised move is best rather than just thoughtlessly playing from memory. 8. As you study theoretical endgames, always keep in mind how the positions can transpose among themselves. In this way, you are able to develop general endgame principles on your own. 9. Identify your most common calculation mistakes (dismissing tactical motifs, unfamiliarity with patterns, and poor visualisation) and work on them. 10. Train your visualisation with the blindfold chess exercises. }  
